To: tradesprior@gmail.com

Post-AGI Alpha Hunt =E2=80=94 new rabbit holes 1) InP epiwafer capacity may=
 be the
hidden bottleneck underneath AI optics

This is stronger than the generic =E2=80=9Cphotonics wins=E2=80=9D thesis. =
On 7 Sep 2026,
IQE reported >40% YoY H1 revenue growth and said it is converting existing
tooling in H2 specifically to raise *Indium Phosphide (InP)* capacity for
significant AI/datacentre demand. IQE also signed a multi-year InP epiwafer
agreement with Tower Semiconductor for 200G/lane and future 400G/lane
optical platforms. Coherent=E2=80=99s 2026 roadmap spans InP lasers, EMLs,
photodiodes and CPO, and Nvidia committed $2B plus multibillion-dollar
purchase commitments/capacity rights to Coherent.

*Alpha hypothesis:* the visible bottleneck is =E2=80=9Coptics=E2=80=9D; the=
 less visible
upstream control point is *epitaxy / InP wafer capacity and qualification*.
If 1.6T/3.2T optics ramp faster than expected, owning qualified InP supply
may have more convexity than the already-crowded transceiver names.

*Candidate exposure:* IQE (AIM: IQE) is unusually small relative to the
importance of the layer. H1 2026 revenue was =C2=A364.6m, adjusted EBITDA =
=C2=A36.0m,
and net cash =C2=A330.2m. Coherent is a much larger, diversified cross-worl=
d
exposure.

*Falsifiers:* silicon photonics/VCSEL/QD-laser architectures reduce InP
intensity faster than optical demand grows; rapid epi capacity conversion
eliminates scarcity; IQE fails customer qualification or converts demand
into low-margin volume.

Sources:
https://www.iqep.com/media/press-releases/2026/2026-interim-results/ ;
https://www.iqep.com/media/press-releases/2026/iqe-and-tower-semiconductor-=
announce-multi-year-inp-epiwafer-supply-agreement/
;
https://www.coherent.com/news/press-releases/nvidia-and-coherent-announce-s=
trategic-partnership
2) =E2=80=9CCapacity reservation rights=E2=80=9D should become a first-clas=
s asset in the
kernel

The recurring pattern across AI infrastructure is no longer simple
ownership of hardware. Nvidia=E2=80=99s Coherent deal explicitly includes *=
future
access and capacity rights*. Data-centre developers are reserving grid
positions, turbine slots and equipment years in advance. Reuters reported
this week that creditors are becoming stricter about AI-infrastructure
projects, increasingly requiring permits and leases before funding; delays
from electricity access, supply chains and local opposition are becoming
credit variables.

*New kernel variable:*
DeliverableCapacity =3D AnnouncedCapacity =C3=97 P(site control) =C3=97 P(p=
ower) =C3=97
P(equipment slot) =C3=97 P(permitting) =C3=97 P(financing)

Then value the *rights* to each scarce slot. Two developers with identical
announced MW can have radically different real option value.

*Alpha implication:* company disclosures about queue positions, deposits,
site control, long-lead purchase agreements and reservation contracts can
be scraped and scored before headline capacity numbers are discounted by
the market.

*Falsifier:* reservation markets become liquid/transferable and fully
priced, or supply expands fast enough that slot scarcity disappears.

Source: Reuters, 8 Sep 2026:
https://www.reuters.com/commentary/breakingviews/ai-construction-crunch-wid=
ens-credit-fault-lines-2026-09-08/
3) AI power bottleneck is migrating from the grid to rotating metal

A less-obvious second derivative of grid scarcity is *onsite generation*.
As developers try to bypass multi-year interconnection queues, gas turbines
themselves become scarce. Current industry reporting points to multi-year
turbine backlogs. Even more interesting: Reuters reported on 9 Sep that
Howmet is seeing additional turbine-component demand from AI-powered data
centres while struggling with the scale of capital expansion needed to
satisfy aerospace + turbine demand.

This creates a cross-world node: *precision castings / superalloy turbine
components* benefit from both aviation and incremental onsite power
generation. GE Aerospace=E2=80=99s $12B acquisition of CPP to secure castin=
g
capacity is powerful revealed-preference evidence that these manufacturing
processes are genuinely difficult to scale.

*Candidate exposure:* Howmet Aerospace is not obscure or cheap, but the
underlying rabbit hole is smaller
casting/forging/superalloy/process-equipment suppliers that sit beneath
both aerospace and power turbines.

*Falsifiers:* data centres pivot decisively to batteries/fuel
cells/nuclear/grid power; turbine OEM capacity ramps faster than component
supply; AI datacentre developers reject gas for permitting/emissions
reasons.

Sources:
https://www.reuters.com/legal/transactional/howmet-ceo-fine-with-ge-aerospa=
ce-deal-working-meet-demand-engine-parts-2026-09-09/
;
https://www.reuters.com/business/aerospace-defense/ge-aerospace-bets-black-=
art-casting-secure-jet-engine-supply-2026-09-09/
4) Glass-core packaging: thickness/warpage may create an equipment
transition before glass becomes mainstream

Today, 10 Sep 2026, Philoptics (161580.KQ) showed 2.0mm-thick, 510=C3=97515=
mm
glass substrates with claimed zero-defect TGV machining and said several
overseas customers had completed technical validation. The reason thicker
glass is becoming relevant is itself causal: AI packages are getting
physically larger as more GPUs/HBM are integrated, which worsens warpage;
thicker glass increases stiffness.

*Alpha chain:*
larger AI package =E2=86=92 warpage problem =E2=86=92 thicker glass cores =
=E2=86=92 harder TGV
drilling =E2=86=92 premium on laser process/yield =E2=86=92 equipment winne=
rs

This is a good example of a *third-order bottleneck*: not =E2=80=9Cglass
substrates,=E2=80=9D but *reliable high-aspect-ratio TGV processing on
thicker/larger panels*.

*Candidates:* Philoptics, LPKF, EO Technics; monitor actual production-tool
orders rather than demos. This could become highly convex if glass-core
adoption crosses from pilot to volume.

*Falsifiers:* organic substrates improve enough; glass adoption slips
beyond 2028; multiple laser vendors commoditize drilling; customer
validation never becomes production orders.

Sources:
https://en.sedaily.com/finance/2026/09/10/philoptics-shows-zero-defect-glas=
s-drilling-on-2mm
; https://view.asiae.co.kr/en/article/2026090710053387061
5) Autonomous science may create a *coordination bottleneck*, not just an
instrument bottleneck

A July 2026 arXiv paper explicitly frames the *validation bottleneck* in
self-driving labs and attacks two constraints: too many experimental rounds
and high cost per experiment. Another March 2026 paper shows mobile robot
chemists can lose throughput simply waiting for humans to access shared
instruments.

This suggests a deeper scarcity migration:
AI hypothesis abundance =E2=86=92 instrument scarcity =E2=86=92 lab schedul=
ing/robot
logistics scarcity =E2=86=92 orchestration layer

So the winner may not only be Thermo Fisher/Bruker/Tecan-type instrument
vendors. There may be a new software/control market for *lab-wide
schedulers that price instrument time, route samples and decide
high-resolution vs surrogate measurements*.

This is analogous to GPU cluster schedulers, but for atoms.

*Tradable angle today:* instrument vendors with open automation APIs and
robot-compatible workflows may gain share because autonomous labs optimize
around interoperability. The really convex exposure may still be
private/too early.

*Falsifier:* labs remain bespoke enough that orchestration never
standardizes, or vendors vertically integrate the scheduler into their own
ecosystems.

Sources: https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04508 ; https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.0842=
0
; https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.03286
6) Strategic-complementarity feedback can create adoption avalanches

The August 2026 revision of the NBER =E2=80=9CInnovation Race=E2=80=9D expe=
riment is more
important than it first looked. ~3,000 firms systematically underestimated
competitors=E2=80=99 AI/robotics adoption. When informed, firms revised bel=
iefs and
increased intended robotics adoption; a 1 percentage point increase in
expected competitor adoption raised intended robotics adoption by 0.732 pp.

That means AI adoption can be *self-exciting*:
competitor adoption =E2=86=92 belief update =E2=86=92 own adoption =E2=86=
=92 competitor belief
update =E2=86=92 =E2=80=A6

This is an endogenous threshold mechanism. Once observed adoption crosses a
visibility threshold, capex can accelerate much faster than smooth
diffusion curves predict.

*Alpha signal:* monitor not just adoption itself, but *visibility of
adoption*: earnings-call mentions of competitor automation, trade-show
deployments, job postings, factory tours, public robot deployments. The
market may model unit demand smoothly while real demand jumps after
competitive panic.

*Potential beneficiaries:* robotics component suppliers with bottleneck
capacity; industrial automation; machine vision; precision
reducers/encoders/force-torque sensors.

*Falsifier:* intentions fail to become orders, ROI remains poor, or
component architectures commoditize.

Source: https://www.nber.org/papers/w34532
Highest-priority additions to the kernel from this run

   1. *Capacity-rights graph:* equipment slots, queue position, permits,
   site control, deposits, reservation contracts.
   2. *Deliverable-vs-announced capacity score:* discount every MW/fab/tool
   pipeline by implementation probability.
   3. *Upstream qualification bottleneck detector:* look for phrases like
   =E2=80=9Ccapacity conversion,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Ccustomer qualification,=
=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Callocation,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Clong-term
   supply agreement,=E2=80=9D especially in tiny upstream suppliers.
   4. *Bottleneck derivative:* when current bottleneck is relieved,
   automatically walk one edge downstream/upstream to find what breaks next=
.
   5. *Adoption avalanche model:* make competitor-visible adoption increase
   the hazard rate of industry-wide adoption rather than keeping diffusion
   exogenous.

Current best rabbit hole from this run

*IQE / InP epitaxy* is the one I would investigate next. It has the desired
shape: a small public company, an upstream layer that multiple optical
architectures need, direct fresh evidence of >40% revenue growth and
capacity conversion, multi-year customer agreements, and a potential shift
from niche compound-semiconductor supplier to constrained AI-infrastructure
input. It is not yet enough to call it a buy: we need current valuation,
customer concentration, real InP capacity share, incremental gross margin,
capex required per wafer, competitor capacity (Coherent/II-VI ecosystem,
Sumitomo, AXT etc.), and architecture substitution risk.

