6) Strategic-complementarity feedback can create adoption avalanches
<p>The August 2026 revision of the NBER =E2=80=9CInnovation Race=E2=80=9D e=
xperiment is more important than it first looked. ~3,000 firms systematical=
ly underestimated competitors=E2=80=99 AI/robotics adoption. When informed,=
 firms revised beliefs and increased intended robotics adoption; a 1 percen=
tage point increase in expected competitor adoption raised intended robotic=
s adoption by 0.732 pp.</p>
<p>That means AI adoption can be <strong>self-exciting</strong>:<br>
<code>competitor adoption =E2=86=92 belief update =E2=86=92 own adoption =
=E2=86=92 competitor belief update =E2=86=92 =E2=80=A6</code></p>
<p>This is an endogenous threshold mechanism. Once observed adoption crosse=
s a visibility threshold, capex can accelerate much faster than smooth diff=
usion curves predict.</p>
<p><strong>Alpha signal:</strong> monitor not just adoption itself, but <st=
rong>visibility of adoption</strong>: earnings-call mentions of competitor =
automation, trade-show deployments, job postings, factory tours, public rob=
ot deployments. The market may model unit demand smoothly while real demand=
 jumps after competitive panic.</p>
<p><strong>Potential beneficiaries:</strong> robotics component suppliers w=
ith bottleneck capacity; industrial automation; machine vision; precision r=
educers/encoders/force-torque sensors.</p>
<p><strong>Falsifier:</strong> intentions fail to become orders, ROI remain=
s poor, or component architectures commoditize.</p>
<p>Source: <a href=3D"https://www.nber.org/papers/w34532">https://www.nber.=
org/papers/w34532</a></p>

